   CSS NOTES: Inline Styles & Specificity

   What is an Inline Style?
   -----------------------
   An inline style is CSS written directly inside an HTML
   element using the style attribute.

   Example:
   <p style="color: red;">Text</p>

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Why Inline Styles Have Highest Priority
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   - Applied directly on the element
   - No selector matching required
   - Targets exactly one element
   - Treated as an explicit instruction by the browser

   Inline styles override:
   - Element selectors (p, div, h1)
   - Class selectors (.text)
   - ID selectors (#main)

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Inline Styles and Specificity (Conceptual)
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Specificity is about how precisely a rule targets
   an element.

   Inline styles are the most precise because they:
   - Apply to one exact element
   - Have no ambiguity
   - Do not depend on CSS selectors

   Therefore, inline styles always win over normal CSS.

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Example Scenario
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   p { color: blue; }
   .text { color: green; }
   #main { color: red; }

   <p class="text" id="main" style="color: purple;">
     Text
   </p>

   Result:
   - Purple color is applied due to inline style.

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Why Inline Styles Are Bad Practice
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   - Break separation of HTML and CSS
   - Hard to maintain and reuse
   - Difficult to override later
   - Cause specificity conflicts

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Important Note
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Inline styles can be overridden only by !important,
   which should be avoided in real projects.

   ---------------------------------------------------------
   One-Line Summary
   ---------------------------------------------------------
   Inline styles override all selector-based CSS rules
   because they directly target the element.

